<title>NETHERLANDS: TABLE - Dutch see 1998 GDP growth of 3.75 percent.</title>
<headline>TABLE - Dutch see 1998 GDP growth of 3.75 percent.</headline>
<dateline>THE HAGUE 1997-07-01</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Dutch government's Central Planning Bureau (CPB) published the following updated forecasts for the Dutch economy on Tuesday:</p>
<p>						1998    1997    1996   1995</p>
<p>    Gross domestic product	    3.75    3.25    2.8    2.1</p>
<p>    Consumer price index		2.0     2.25    2.1    2.0</p>
<p>    Private consumption		 2.5     2.75    3.0    2.1</p>
<p>    Private residential investment  1.5     5.5    (0.8)   1.6</p>
<p>    Private nonresidential inv.     4.0     5.0     8.6    10.1</p>
<p>    Exports				 8.25    6.5     4.1    8.6</p>
<p>    Imports				 6.25    5.5     6.4    8.3</p>
<p>    Contract wages			3.0     2.5     1.6    1.4</p>
<p>    Unemployment rate		   6.0     6.75    7.6    8.3</p>
<p>    Current account surplus	   6.0     5.5     4.7    5.1</p>
<p>    Dollar rate in guilders	   1.85    1.90    1.69   1.61</p>
<p>    10yr Dutch govt bond yield(pct) 5.75    5.75    6.2    6.9</p>
<p>    EMU deficit			   1.75    2.0     2.8    4.0</p>
<p>    EMU debt				71.5    73.5    77.9   79.7</p>
<p>NOTES -- All figures show real percentage changes versus previous year unless otherwise stated in table or below. CBS rounds off estimates to the nearest 0.25 percent. Exports exclude energy.</p>
<p>Unemployment is stated according to standardised international definition as a percentage of the labour force.</p>
<p>Current account surplus is expressed as a percentage of GDP. 1998 EMU deficit/debt estimates based on assumption 1998 budget will allocate any windfalls equally between deficit reduction and tax relief.</p>
<p>-- Andrew Clark, Amsterdam newsroom +31 20 504 5009</p>
</text>
